  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Quick Summary</h2>
    <p>Thanksgiving spread across two days. Turkey gets marinated in shio-koji (Japanese fermented rice) for extra flavor and tenderness. Thursday is prep and a light Japanese-fusion dinner. Friday is the full feast.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>What's Happening</h2>
    <p>I'm doing Thanksgiving a little differently this year—splitting it across two days and trying out koji on the turkey.</p>

    <p><strong>Thursday (Thanksgiving Day)</strong>: Bake dinner rolls and a cheesecake for tomorrow. Make turkey stock from the carcass (which also got some koji for extra umami). Have a light Japanese-American dinner while the stock simmers—turkey broth soba, edamame, salad with crispy turkey skin.</p>

    <p><strong>Friday (Turkey Day)</strong>: The actual feast. Turkey that's been marinating in shio-koji for 36 hours, then air-dried for crispy skin. All the usual sides—Brussels sprouts with sausage, hasselback potatoes, stuffing, gravy. Plus the rolls and desserts from yesterday.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Why Koji?</h2>
    <p>Shio-koji is fermented rice with salt. The enzymes break down proteins, which makes meat more tender and adds a lot of umami flavor. It's basically a marinade that does more work than regular salt water. Also I think it sounds interesting.</p>

    <h3>The Process:</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Made shio-koji 6 days ahead (kept it in a sous-vide bath at 91°F to speed it up)</li>
      <li>Butchered turkey into parts, coated in koji, vacuum sealed for 36 hours</li>
      <li>Wiped it off, let the skin air-dry in the fridge for 36 hours</li>
      <li>Roast on Friday</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

