  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>The Timeline</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>6 days before</strong>: Start shio-koji fermentation (sous-vide at 91°F)</li>
      <li><strong>4 days before</strong>: Turkey starts thawing in fridge</li>
      <li><strong>2 days before</strong>: Butcher turkey, apply koji marinade, vacuum seal</li>
      <li><strong>1 day before</strong>: Remove from koji, start air-drying skin</li>
      <li><strong>Thursday</strong>: Bake rolls and cheesecake, make stock, have fusion dinner</li>
      <li><strong>Friday 12pm</strong>: Turkey in oven</li>
      <li><strong>Friday 2pm</strong>: Eat</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Everything's planned backwards from eating at 2pm on Friday.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Notes</h2>
    <p>The two-day thing makes it less chaotic. Stock is done and cold when I need it for gravy. Bread is already baked. Dessert is made. Turkey just needs to roast.</p>

    <p>The Japanese-fusion Thursday dinner uses the stock while it's cooking, so nothing goes to waste and it bridges the two days together.</p>

    <p>The gravy uses a mix of thickeners (roux, potato starch, gelatin, xanthan) because I wanted it to have body without being heavy or pasty.</p>
  </div>

